Pair indexing packs a sequence of maps into one map via Cantor pairing of fibre indices with values.

**Definition (`pairIndexed`).**
\[
(\mathrm{pairIndexed}\,f_s)\,x
=\{k\mid\exists n\,m.\,k=\langle n,m\rangle\land m\in f_s(n)(x)\}.
\]

**Theorem (`pairIndexed.eq_top`).** $\mathrm{pairIndexed}\,f_s\,x=\top$ if and only if every $f_s(n)(x)$ equals $\top$.

*Proof.* Every pair code belongs to the packed value precisely when each fibre is universal; surjectivity of pairing recovers the fibres. ∎

**Theorem (`pairIndexed.isScottContinuous`).** If each $f_s(n)$ is Scott-continuous, so is $\mathrm{pairIndexed}\,f_s$.

*Proof.* Membership of a pair code pulls a Scott-union witness from the corresponding fibre and reassembles under the packed Scott union; monotonicity of each fibre closes the converse. ∎
